// MyObject class (with private methods)
class MyObject {
    public int add(int x, int y) {
        return x + y;
    }

    public String greeting(String name) {
        return "Hello " + name;
    }
}

// MyClass2 class with public and static methods
class MyClass2 {
    // Public method
    public static int addNumbers(int a, int b) {
        return a + b;
    }

    // Another public method
    public String greetingStatic(String name) {
        return "Hello " + name;
    }
}

// MyClass class with private and protected methods
class MyClass {
    private int addPrivate(int x, int y) {
        return x + y;
    }

    private String addProtected(String name) {
        return "Hello " + name;
    }

    public static int multiply(int x, int y) {
        return x * y;
    }
}

public class MyClassStatic {
    // Public method
    public static int addNumbers(int a, int b) {
        return a + b;
    }

    public static String greetingStatic(String name) {
        return "Hello " + name;
    }
}
```

Note that in Java, the `MyObject` and `MyClass2` classes have private methods with access modifiers (private), which can only be accessed within the same class. The `MyClass2` class has a protected method (`addProtected`) that can also be accessed outside the same class. However, the `MyClassStatic` class is not considered to be a public or private method because it's not defined inside any other class and does not access any fields or methods of another class.

The output of this code will be:

